==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{q|South Asia only}} {{alt|en|nala|nalla|nallah|nulla}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|bn|নালা}}, {{bor|en|hi|नाला}}, from {{der|en|sa|नाडी}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|South Asia}} A [[stream]]-bed, [[ravine]], or other [[watercourse]]; a [[drain]] for rain or floodwater. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1849|author=Brigadier Lockwood|title=Report of 2nd Cavalry Division at Battle of Goojerat
|passage=About this time a large [[gole]] of horsemen came on towards me, and I proposed to charge; but as they turned at once from the fire of the guns, and as there was a '''nullah''' in front, I refrained from advancing after them.}}
#* {{RQ:Forster Passage to India|passage=It was just after the exit from a bridge; the animal had probably come up out of the '''nullah'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|date=2023-11-30|author=Oliver Franklin-Wallis|title=Inside India’s Gargantuan Mission to Clean the Ganges River|work=Wired|url=https://www.wired.com/story/india-ganges-river-clean-project/|issn=1059-1028|passage=Until a few years ago, much of the city’s sewage was released untreated into the Ganges via public drains, or '''''nullahs''''', which discharged along the same bank as the ghats, where people habitually bathe.}}
# {{lb|en|Hong Kong}} An [[open-air]], [[concrete]]-lined [[channel]] for [[drain]]ing rain or wastewater; a [[storm drain]].

